<%# The attention band: what needs you, then where the money actually went.
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). See the UI design spec §4.2.

    Three kinds of problem, in descending loudness: an overdrawn account (no other band can
    report it — see HomePresenter#overdrawn_accounts), a pool with no account that is
    actually asking for money this period (nothing can fund it, and it is deliberately
    absent from the waterfall below), then the envelopes whose own status needs attention,
    in the priority order the user ranked them in. `uniq` because a pool can be both
    unassigned and overdrawn.

    `orphan_pools_owed`, NOT `orphan_pools` — the middle term is the one that changed and
    this is why. Every orphan used to count here regardless of status, so a healthy user
    with five account-less savings goals opened the root route on "You're covered this
    period" above "5 things need you", with five rows saying nothing can fund goals that
    were asking for nothing. An account-less savings pool is the ORDINARY shape until Plan
    3's backfill, so that was not an edge; it was the common screen.

    `orphan_pools_owed` is exactly the set whose ask #orphan_required is made of, so this
    heading now counts the same pools the standing band's clause above already names — one
    set, said twice, rather than two sets a reader has to reconcile. And it loses nothing:
    an orphan that is ALSO in trouble on its own terms (overdrawn, overdue, behind) is
    already in `attention_pools` and still counted, still labelled "no account · …" by
    `pool_problem_label`. What drops out is only the quiet orphan asking for nothing — and
    the orphans band below still lists every one of them under a heading that says these
    have no account, which is where "these have no account" belongs. %>
<% orphans = presenter.orphan_pools %>
<% problems = (presenter.overdrawn_accounts + presenter.orphan_pools_owed + presenter.attention_pools).uniq %>
<% rows = presenter.waterfall %>

<%# The cutoff marks where the cash ran out, which is AFTER the last pool that received any
    of it: a pool funded $355 of $400 did get money, and the spec's §4.2 waterfall draws the
    line beneath it. So it goes before the first pool that got nothing — or at the end, when
    the money ran out part-way through the last row and nothing below it exists.

    Only ever with a single account. Each account drains its own pot, so with several there
    is no single moment the money ran out: a pool in Ally funded at zero would print the line
    above rows in Checking that were funded in full. The per-row figures carry it instead.
    Grouping the waterfall by account is the real answer and belongs to a later plan. %>
<%# `covered?` in the guard as well as `accounts.one?`: the waterfall now renders on a covered
    period too (see below), and there the index finds nothing, falls back to `rows.length`,
    and draws "ran out here · $0.00 unfunded" under the last row of a screen where nothing
    ran out at all. There is no cutoff when the money reached everything. %>
<% single_account_shortfall = presenter.accounts.one? && !presenter.covered? %>
<% cutoff = single_account_shortfall ? (rows.index { |row| row[:funded].zero? && row[:short].positive? } || rows.length) : nil %>

<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" aria-labelledby="attention-heading">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 id="attention-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <% if problems.any? %>
        <%= pluralize(problems.size, "thing") %> need<%= "s" if problems.one? %> you
      <% elsif presenter.covered? %>
        Nothing needs you
      <% else %>
        <%# Real state, not a corner: every rate envelope reads `left_to_spend`, so a period
            can be short with no pool flagged. "Nothing needs you" above a gap is the exact
            failure this band exists to prevent. %>
        Nothing is flagged, but you're still short
      <% end %>
    </h3>
  </div>

  <% problems.each do |pool| %>
    <% status = presenter.status_for(pool) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 <%= "bg-status-danger-light" if status.state == :overdrawn %>">
      <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline">
        <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= pool.name %></span>
        <span class="text-sm text-status-danger">
          <%= pool_problem_label(status, orphan: orphans.include?(pool)) %>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Whenever there is a gap OR something needs you.

      It used to be the gap alone, and that suppressed the plan on exactly the screen that
      needed it: `won't make it · Aug 18` rendered under "You're covered this period" with no
      way to see that this period's distribution funds the bill in full. PoolStatus describes
      the pool NOW, the waterfall describes the plan, and showing the warning while hiding its
      own resolution is the worst combination of the two.

      Still silent when nothing is short and nothing is flagged, which is principle 3 intact:
      there is no consequence to report on a screen with no problems on it.

      And `rows.any?` first, because "something needs you" is NOT the same question as "is
      there a plan to show". Two of the three kinds of problem — an overdrawn account and an
      account-less pool — have no waterfall row at all, and the zero-need reject can empty the
      list outright, so this heading could render over nothing. On a money screen an empty
      headed band reads as data that failed to load. %>
  <% if rows.any? && (!presenter.covered? || problems.any?) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-4 border-t border-gray-200" aria-labelledby="waterfall-heading">
      <%# Sentence case, not the `uppercase` treatment this codebase gives short labels like
          "Current Balance": that renders as WHERE YOUR MONEY WENT, which shouts a whole
          sentence — and it is the literal copy the design spec asks for. %>
      <%# Present tense, not "went": this band has never described a completed distribution —
          it describes what the NEXT one would do. Past tense was tolerable while it only
          appeared on a short screen, where a shortfall reads like something that already
          happened to you; it now renders on covered screens too, above a plan that plainly
          has not run. The design spec is being corrected to match. %>
      <p id="waterfall-heading" class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500 mb-2">Where your money goes</p>

      <%# Two groups rather than a flag inside one loop: the cutoff is then written once and
          renders correctly at either edge — before every row when the first pool got nothing,
          and after the last row when the money ran out inside it. A nil cutoff (several
          accounts) leaves one group, and no line. %>
      <% groups = cutoff ? [rows.first(cutoff), rows.drop(cutoff)] : [rows] %>
      <% groups.each_with_index do |group, index| %>
        <% if index == 1 %>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-dashed border-status-danger my-2 pt-2 text-sm text-status-danger font-medium">
            — ran out here · <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> unfunded —
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <% group.each do |row| %>
          <div class="flex justify-between text-sm py-1">
            <span class="text-gray-900"><%= row[:pool].name %></span>
            <span class="text-gray-600">
              <%= number_to_currency(row[:funded]) %> of <%= number_to_currency(row[:needed]) %>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
